---
/**
 * What eXir is, who builds it, and how to reach or support them.
 *
 * Laid out as a drink page is: the cards that say who and where in the left
 * column, the prose in the reading column beside them. Everything here is
 * vocabulary the site already has — the body grid, the glass card, the About
 * panel's reading measure — with one new control, the support link, which is
 * the outlined chip carried in the accent wash.
 *
 * §20's approved copy ships as written. The section below headed "On alcohol"
 * is that text verbatim and is not to be reworded.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PayPalMark from '../assets/paypal.svg';
import CoffeeMark from '../assets/buy-me-a-coffee.svg';
import { published, site } from '../lib/content/site.ts';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const resolved = await site();
const drinks = published(resolved);
const ingredients = [...resolved.ingredients.values()];

/** Counted from the content, never typed — the same rule as everything else. */
const counts = [
  { label: 'Drinks', value: drinks.length },
  { label: 'Ingredients', value: ingredients.filter((i) => i.kind !== 'preparation').length },
  { label: 'Preparations', value: ingredients.filter((i) => i.kind === 'preparation').length },
  { label: 'Glasses', value: resolved.glassware.size },
];

const contact = [
  { label: 'Personal site', href: 'https://kiarashfa.github.io/website/', note: 'kiarashfa.github.io' },
  { label: 'Email', href: 'mailto:kiarashfa@gmail.com', note: 'kiarashfa@gmail.com' },
  { label: 'GitHub', href: 'https://github.com/kiarashfa', note: 'github.com/kiarashfa' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About eXir"
  description="What eXir is, how its numbers are worked out, who builds it and where its figures come from."
>
  <section class="wrap page-head">
    <h1>About eXir</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A drinks encyclopedia where every figure is computed rather than typed, built and kept in the
      open by one person. Free, ad-free, login-free and cookie-free.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="wrap body-grid">
    <aside class="col-stack">
      <div class="left-col">
        <div class="card glass">
          <h2 class="card-title">Who made this</h2>
          <div class="author-head">
            <img
              class="author-avatar"
              src={`${base}/images/author.webp`}
              alt="Kiarash Farajzadehahary"
              width="64"
              height="64"
            />
            <div>
              <div class="author-name">Kiarash Farajzadehahary</div>
              <div class="author-role">Scientist · developer · designer</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="author-bio">
            I kept doing the same arithmetic on the back of an envelope — how strong is this once
            the ice has done its work, how much water does a batch of twelve need, how much of that
            bottle am I actually going to use. eXir is what I wanted instead: one page per drink
            that does its own sums and cannot contradict itself. I build it in the open, on my own
            time, and I would genuinely like to hear when something here is wrong.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card glass">
          <h2 class="card-title">Contact</h2>
          <ul class="contact-list">
            {contact.map((item) => (
              <li>
                {item.href.startsWith('mailto:') ? (
                  <a href={item.href}>{item.label}</a>
                ) : (
                  <a href={item.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{item.label}</a>
                )}
                <span class="ing-meta">{item.note}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card glass">
          <h2 class="card-title">Support</h2>
          <p class="support-note">
            eXir is free, ad-free and login-free, and stays that way — if it saved you a round, you
            can put something toward the next hundred drinks.
          </p>
          <div class="support-links">
            <a
              class="support-link"
              href="https://www.paypal.com/donate/?hosted_button_id=S3BD5XFBMMWSJ"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
            >
              <PayPalMark />
              <span>Donate · PayPal</span>
            </a>
            <a
              class="support-link"
              href="https://www.buymeacoffee.com/kiarashfa"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener"
            >
              <CoffeeMark />
              <span>Buy Me a Coffee</span>
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card glass">
          <h2 class="card-title">Where it stands</h2>
          <div class="spec-grid">
            {counts.map((count) => (
              <div class="spec-cell">
                <span class="spec-key">{count.label}</span>
                <span class="spec-val">{count.value}</span>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
          <p class="disclaimer">
            Counted from the published content at build time. The catalogue is being written; what
            is here is written rather than generated, and each page's figures are checked by the
            same build that publishes it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </aside>

    <div class="col-stack">
      <div class="about-body about-page-body">
        <h2>No number here is typed by hand</h2>
        <p>
          Every quantity, dilution, final volume, strength, standard-drink figure, sugar figure and
          energy figure on this site is computed from structured data. Amounts are authored in
          millilitres and grams; ounces, temperatures in Fahrenheit, counts of dashes and limes, and
          every total are display layers worked out at the moment you read them.
        </p>
        <p>
          That means a page cannot contradict itself. Change the drink count, the service mode or
          the units, and the arithmetic follows — including the water that shaking or stirring adds,
          which is an ingredient in a batched drink and an accident in an unbatched one.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a food site that discipline is a purity principle. For a drinks site it is the
          product, because the arithmetic <em>is</em> the craft: dilution, batching, strength and
          brew ratio are calculations working bartenders do constantly and home drinkers get wrong.
        </p>

        <h2>What is measured, what is modelled</h2>
        <p>
          A figure taken from a dataset or a producer's own label is stated. A figure that comes out
          of a model is marked with a dotted underline and a tilde, and that marker means one thing
          everywhere on the site: the true value is not on this page.
        </p>
        <p>
          Dilution is the main one. How much water a drink takes on from ice is modelled, not
          measured, and every figure descending from it — the final volume, the strength, the sugar
          per litre — is marked accordingly. The fraction is always a fraction of the poured volume
          <em>before</em> any ice, and the site says so wherever it appears, because published
          figures disagree with one another on precisely that point.
        </p>
        <p>
          Bitterness is the other. There is no bitterness figure on any bottle and no unit for it,
          so it is an editorial judgement, and it is drawn differently from the three bars beside it
          for exactly that reason. Difficulty is the same kind of thing.
        </p>

        <h2>Where the figures come from</h2>
        <p>
          Composition data comes from the United States Department of Agriculture's FoodData
          Central, which is public domain, from producers' own published figures where a product is
          proprietary and nothing else carries it, and from Open Food Facts for branded products
          neither covers. Every Form on every ingredient page names its source, and the whole list
          with its licences is on the <a href={`${base}/attributions/`}>sources</a> page.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nothing computed is ever imported. A third party's arithmetic for a finished drink's
          strength or calories would undo the only thing that makes this site worth building.
        </p>
        <p>
          Photography comes from Wikimedia Commons, curated by hand and credited on each image
          itself — the small &ldquo;i&rdquo; opens the file, the author, the licence and what was
          done to it. Cropping and grading makes a derivative work, which is why that record travels
          with the picture rather than living in an index.
        </p>

        <h2>On alcohol</h2>
        <p>
          Roughly half of what eXir documents contains alcohol, and the site states how much — in
          grams of pure alcohol, and in the standard-drink measures used in the United States and
          the United Kingdom. Those figures are here because a drink's strength is a fact about the
          drink, and a reference work that computes everything else while staying quiet about that
          one would be making a choice.
        </p>
        <p>
          They are calculations, not measurements. Each is derived from the stated strength of the
          bottles a recipe names, adjusted for the water the method adds. A different bottle, a
          longer stir, or a heavier pour changes the answer, and the site marks every figure that
          descends from an estimate.
        </p>
        <p>
          eXir does not recommend drinking, does not suggest quantities, and holds no view on what
          anyone's limit should be. Guidance on that comes from health authorities in your own
          country, who publish it freely and revise it as the evidence changes. Legal drinking ages
          differ too, and the site assumes nothing about where you are. Every drink here that can be
          made without alcohol says so, and a large part of the catalogue never contained any.
        </p>
        <p>
          If alcohol is causing harm to you or to someone close to you, help exists and it is local.
          National health services and support organisations in your own country are the place to
          start.
        </p>

        <h2>What it keeps, and where</h2>
        <p>
          There is no backend, no account and nothing to sign in to. Whatever the site remembers —
          your shelf in <a href={`${base}/my-bar/`}>My&nbsp;Bar</a>, your
          <a href={`${base}/plan/`}>plan</a>, your units, your theme — stays in your own browser and
          never leaves it. That also means it lives in one browser on one device, and the site
          cannot move it for you.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
